dwc2: dcd_edpt_iso_activate() disables an IN endpoint with the USB interrupt masked

dcd_edpt_iso_activate() runs in the task, for SET_INTERFACE, and first disables the
endpoint through edpt_disable(). The USB interrupt's handle_incomplete_iso_in() can
disable the same IN endpoint at the same moment, after its retries are used up. Both
then set SNAK and EPDIS and wait for EPDISD. The ISR clears the flag first, and the
task waits for it forever.

The fix holds the usbd spin lock (in_isr=false, so the USB interrupt is masked) around
the IN endpoint's disable in dcd_edpt_iso_activate(). OUT endpoints don't need it:
the ISR never disables them.

From lost-tty/CHOMPI (MIT), where it was found. Upstream master (4c195b7) has
reworked incomplete-ISO-IN handling with iso_retry, but iso_activate still takes no
lock.

Upstream candidate: yes, once checked against master's new incomplete-ISO-IN code.

--- a/src/portable/synopsys/dwc2/dcd_dwc2.c	2026-10-14 13:10:53.446221642 +0200
+++ b/src/portable/synopsys/dwc2/dcd_dwc2.c	2026-10-14 13:16:05.623623043 +0200
@@ -647,7 +653,16 @@
 
 bool dcd_edpt_iso_activate(uint8_t rhport,  tusb_desc_endpoint_t const * p_endpoint_desc) {
   // Disable EP to clear potential incomplete transfers
+  // FRIZZ (patches/0002): an IN endpoint with the USB interrupt masked, so handle_incomplete_iso_in() can't
+  // disable the same endpoint in the middle of it (this runs in the task, on SET_INTERFACE)
+  const bool is_in = tu_edpt_dir(p_endpoint_desc->bEndpointAddress) == TUSB_DIR_IN;
+  if (is_in) {
+    usbd_spin_lock(false);
+  }
   edpt_disable(rhport, p_endpoint_desc->bEndpointAddress, false);
+  if (is_in) {
+    usbd_spin_unlock(false);
+  }
   edpt_activate(rhport, p_endpoint_desc);
   return true;
 }
